<v Rohan Kulkarni>Right, right.</v>

14
00:01:29.694 --> 00:01:40.483
<v Ananya Desai>Everything lands as raw messages in Postgres. Neon, with pgvector, so embeddings sit in the same database. I really don't want a separate vector store in Phase 1.</v>

15
00:01:41.171 --> 00:01:43.558
<v Rohan Kulkarni>One less bill. I like it.</v>

16
00:01:44.793 --> 00:01:58.724
<v Ananya Desai>Then there are two model passes. A cheap model screens every message, basically, is there anything interesting here, yes or no. And only the yes ones go to the stronger model, which extracts entities, facts and commitments.</v>

17
00:01:59.635 --> 00:02:04.958
<v Rohan Kulkarni>And the commitments are the promises thing. The "I'll send it by Friday" thing.</v>

18
00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:19.085
<v Ananya Desai>Exactly. Each commitment gets an owner, a counterparty, a due date, and a status. Open, done, missed, cancelled. And every status change has to point to a source message, otherwise we're just hallucinating with confidence.</v>

19
00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:23.169
<v Rohan Kulkarni>That's literally the pitch. Citations or it didn't happen.</v>

20
00:02:23.412 --> 00:02:33.208
<v Ananya Desai>And the facts are time-stamped, so if something changes, like a price, we keep the old value with a valid-from, valid-to instead of overwriting it.</v>

